1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,638
Every experimentation tool helps you start an A/B test.

2
00:00:03,638 --> 00:00:05,858
Finishing one is still manual work.

3
00:00:06,117 --> 00:00:08,462
After you end an experiment in Flagsmith,

4
00:00:08,462 --> 00:00:12,696
you set the flag to the winner and clean up the losing variations by hand.

5
00:00:12,696 --> 00:00:14,355
That is how flags accumulate.

6
00:00:14,617 --> 00:00:17,529
Last Call is a small agent for that last step.

7
00:00:17,529 --> 00:00:22,340
It reads the result, proposes one pull request, and after a human merges it,

8
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:23,606
it finishes the job.

9
00:00:23,850 --> 00:00:27,879
This is Trailmix, a demo app with two running Flagsmith experiments.

10
00:00:27,879 --> 00:00:30,604
Half of the visitors see a long invite button,

11
00:00:30,850 --> 00:00:32,811
and the other half see a short one.

12
00:00:32,811 --> 00:00:35,501
Each click is sent to Flagsmith as a real event.

13
00:00:35,750 --> 00:00:41,049
In Flagsmith Cloud the experiment is still running, with 1,401 identities,

14
00:00:41,049 --> 00:00:42,410
and short is ahead.

15
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:48,075
last-call review reads every running experiment through the Flagsmith CLI and gives each one a verdict.

16
00:00:48,317 --> 00:00:51,439
Invite button copy passes five checks in order:

17
00:00:51,439 --> 00:00:56,554
sample ratio, quarantined identities, minimum sample, chance to beat control,

18
00:00:56,554 --> 00:00:59,078
and a credible interval clear of zero.

19
00:00:59,078 --> 00:00:59,610
Verdict:

20
00:00:59,610 --> 00:01:00,340
ship short.

21
00:01:00,583 --> 00:01:04,926
Pricing headline is at 64.5% and its interval crosses zero.

22
00:01:04,926 --> 00:01:08,975
Last Call says keep running, and opens no pull request.

23
00:01:09,217 --> 00:01:13,561
last-call propose turns the ship verdict into a branch and a pull request.

24
00:01:13,561 --> 00:01:15,733
Nothing has changed in Flagsmith yet.

25
00:01:15,983 --> 00:01:18,109
The pull request body is the verdict:

26
00:01:18,109 --> 00:01:21,672
the numbers from Flagsmith's Bayesian results, and the checks.

27
00:01:21,917 --> 00:01:26,069
It says exactly what merging will do, and lists what it left for a human.

28
00:01:26,317 --> 00:01:28,847
The diff deletes the losing branch of the code,

29
00:01:29,083 --> 00:01:33,223
and adds a decision file that pins the experiment, the flag and the winner.

30
00:01:33,483 --> 00:01:35,124
A human reads it and merges.

31
00:01:35,124 --> 00:01:36,648
The merge is the approval.

32
00:01:38,083 --> 00:01:39,988
The merge starts a GitHub Action.

33
00:01:39,988 --> 00:01:41,892
Eighteen seconds after the merge,

34
00:01:41,892 --> 00:01:45,873
one complete call through the Flagsmith CLI has ended the experiment.

35
00:01:46,117 --> 00:01:48,142
In Flagsmith it now reads completed,

36
00:01:48,142 --> 00:01:51,012
with short rolled out to everyone on the same flag.

37
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:55,654
New visitors in this environment now get the winner, and the losing code is gone.

38
00:01:55,917 --> 00:01:57,823
Running apply again does nothing.

39
00:01:57,823 --> 00:02:01,635
If the result had changed since the pull request, it would refuse.

40
00:02:01,883 --> 00:02:02,701
What is real:

41
00:02:02,701 --> 00:02:06,600
Flagsmith Cloud on the free plan, the experiments, the events,

42
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:08,864
the pull request and the Action run.

43
00:02:08,864 --> 00:02:09,996
What is simulated:

44
00:02:09,996 --> 00:02:10,814
the visitors.

45
00:02:10,814 --> 00:02:14,272
Traffic comes from a script, and every verdict says so.

46
00:02:14,517 --> 00:02:15,136
Last Call.

47
00:02:15,136 --> 00:02:17,241
Finish your Flagsmith experiments.

48
00:02:17,241 --> 00:02:20,336
The code and this exact run are in the repository.
